## Rules for a fake app

Level Up Your Kid was built for one five-minute investor demo, and the brief says so. iOS only. Five screens at most. No backend, just local storage. Hardcoded data that looks like 90 days of real use. It has to work in airplane mode.

Two more docs set the rules. A "don't do" list banned real API calls, auth, databases, cloud services and state libraries. An anti-patterns list banned what makes a demo look fake: lorem ipsum, stock photos, random progress numbers, a generic "Welcome, User!"

So the dashboard shows a parent at Level 12 on a 47-day streak with 3,750 XP. Every one of those numbers is invented, so it reads like a family three months in. The PRD's retention and conversion targets were goals too. Nothing was measured.

## Borrowing Duolingo's look

I didn't give the parenting app its own identity. I borrowed Duolingo's. The creative direction says "Duolingo meets modern parenting" and "Apple meets Nintendo." The palette is Duolingo's green, blue, purple and orange. Buttons are chunky with hard drop shadows, corners are 12px, touch targets are 44px, and content stops at 400px wide.

The tone rules come in pairs: "friendly but not childish," "scientific but not clinical," "encouraging but not preachy." None of that palette is mine.

