## One file for every picture

Every illustration and photo in the parenting app comes from one TOML manifest. It compiles to a TypeScript asset map, and a CLI generates the files from the manifest's prompts: FLUX Kontext for images, Veo 3 Fast for video, both through Replicate. A script checks the manifest for anything not generated yet. A 355-line style dictionary keeps the prompts consistent. The manifest says what to make, and the dictionary says how it should look.

It made about 14 illustrations for domains, onboarding, personas and badges, plus persona backgrounds, family hero photos and milestone photos. It also made the testimonial profile photos. Those are AI-generated people, and anything shown here gets labeled that way. I reused the manifest approach for the health coach mock.

{% Gallery images=[{src: "/images/projects/taco-labs/badge-color-master.webp", alt: "Generated badge illustration: a rainbow medal labeled Color Mastery, Completed"}, {src: "/images/projects/taco-labs/badge-milestone-collector.webp", alt: "Generated badge illustration: a green shield with a treasure chest and a Milestone Achieved banner"}, {src: "/images/projects/taco-labs/badge-social-butterfly.webp", alt: "Generated badge illustration: a pink round badge with a butterfly and the words Social Skills"}] columns="3" caption="Badges generated from the manifest's prompts. All AI-generated." /%}

## Testing with made-up people

The persona testing bot was a tool for the label, not a product. A planner model turns a synthetic persona card into test goals. A vision-language model then drives a headless browser through Playwright, deciding what to click from screenshots.

LoveStory and ARMA had more real code behind them. LoveStory has a PDF pipeline for a 16-page 6x9 book, with 12, 17 and 10 passing tests across its suites. ARMA was FastAPI with a terminal UI, Postgres on Neon and Exa for search, and its commits span six days in August 2025.

Taco Labs is retired. The parenting app never had a backend, by design. ARMA got a real database, but nothing in its repo shows a launch.
